# No end-of-line conversion, in either direction, for anything in this branch.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# `tools/build_addon_zip.py` writes each member by reading the path on disk. So
# the archive carries whatever git put in the working tree, and without this
# file that is decided by the developer's `core.autocrlf` rather than by the
# commit. Measured on the machine this was written on, with autocrlf=true: two
# of three sampled members did not carry their committed bytes. The same
# revision built on a differently-configured machine therefore produces a
# different zip and a different sha256 -- and the published checksum is supposed
# to pin the bytes, so that discipline was resting on a local setting.
#
# `-text` rather than `text=auto eol=lf`. Normalising the whole tree to LF would
# be the usual choice and is the wrong one here: the vendored copy is pinned to
# an upstream commit and VENDORED.md states that verbatim is "a measured
# property of the bytes rather than a claim about the copy command". Upstream is
# NOT uniform -- `db.py`, `export.py` and `remote/provider.py` arrived LF while
# the rest of the package arrived CRLF -- so a tree-wide normalisation would
# silently rewrite most of it and make a diff against upstream meaningless. The
# target for each file is whatever upstream holds for that file, and `-text`
# is what lets each one keep it.
#
# The gh-pages branch carries the same rule for the neighbouring reason: the
# bytes it serves are digested and Kodi refuses the repository on a one-byte
# disagreement. Here the bytes are digested one step earlier, on the way into
# the archive.
#
# `tests/test_build_zip.py::test_every_member_carries_the_committed_bytes` is
# what holds this. Deleting this file turns it red.
* -text
